  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nLight rain showers continue from areas around Muddy Gap to \nnorthwest Natrona County as of 08Z (1AM) this morning, with \nadditional showers near Dubois and Pavillion in the Wind River \nBasin. North to northwest winds with gusts of 30 to 40 mph have \nalso developed across areas east of the Divide in the last hour \nor so as the main sfc low has now moved over western portions of\nSD/NE. This trend (of winds and precipitation) will continue \nthrough the rest of the day today as the main storm system \ncontinues its eastward progression over the Northern/Central \nPlains. The highest amounts of precipitation will occur through \nthe morning hours (focused over areas east of the Divide as well\nas Sweetwater County) as most of the supporting upper level \ndynamics will have shifted well to the east by 18Z. Natrona and \nsouthern portions of Johnson County are expected to receive the \nhighest amounts, ranging between 0.20 to 0.50\". Winds will \nincrease over western portions by 12Z, with gusts of 25 to 35 \nmph, before decreasing after 18Z. At the same time, gusts of 50 \nto 60 mph will develop over northern portions of Johnson County \nand continue through much of the day. This remains in line with \nthe current High Wind Warning for northern Johnson County that \nis in effect through 3PM. Snowfall amounts remain largely \nunchanged, with Winter Weather Advisories for the east slopes of\nthe Wind River and Bighorn Mountains in effect through 5PM. \nAdvisory amounts of snow (6\") for Casper amounts are still on \nthe marginal side with confidence remaining too low to issue an \nAdvisory at this time. Overall, it will be a cloudy and windy \nday across the CWA today with daytime highs in the 40s. \n\nWinds will decrease through the evening as the gradient begins \nto weaken and the main storm system continues its eastward \nprogression. Gusts up to 35 mph will continue over much of \nJohnson County through the night. Clouds will also begin to \nscatter out from west to east in the evening. Possible fog \nformation will be a concern Tuesday morning as a result, \nespecially for areas east of the Divide. Precipitation amounts \ntoday will likely help drive this from occurring. \n\nConditions are expected to remain dry through the rest of the \nweek. An area of high pressure will settle over MT Tuesday and \nkeep temperatures at or slightly below normal and winds light. \nThis high center will also keep a weak Pacific system to the \nsouth over UT/CO Wednesday, with the main impact being mainly in\nthe form of cloudy skies over southern/southwestern portions. \nThe pattern briefly becomes more active by Thursday as another \nweak trough moves over the Northern Rockies and a second cold \nfront clips northeastern portions of the state on Friday. High \nwinds look possible again over northern Johnson County in the \nwake of this front, with a 110 to 120kt jet supporting the \nfront. Wind looks to be the main impact with this front for now \nwith conditions remaining dry through the weekend as a strong \nridge moves over the Northern Rockies into Alberta/Saskatchewan.",
